 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. The voluntary: what makes an act human",html:`<p>Thomas distinguishes <em>acts of a man</em> (scratching your head absent-mindedly) from <em>human acts</em> (chosen, with knowledge). Only the second are moral.</p>
<div class="quote">"Now in order for a thing to be done for an end, some knowledge of the end is necessary."<span class="src">ST I-II q.6 a.1</span></div>
<p>So the voluntary needs two things: an intrinsic principle (the act comes from within) and knowledge of the end. Hence the four classic impediments to voluntariness (q.6 aa.5–8): <strong>violence</strong> (external force), <strong>fear</strong> (makes acts \"mixed\": voluntary simply, involuntary in a respect, q.6 a.6), <strong>concupiscence</strong> (passion, which increases rather than decreases voluntariness unless it removes reason, q.6 a.7), and <strong>ignorance</strong> (which causes involuntariness when it is antecedent and not culpable, q.6 a.8).</p>
<div class="co case"><div class="co-h">Case · The captain in the storm</div><p>Aristotle's and Thomas's example: a captain throws cargo overboard in a storm to save the ship. He does it voluntarily (he chooses it here and now), yet involuntarily in a respect (he would not do it apart from the danger). That is a \"mixed\" act.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("According to I-II q.6, what two things make an act voluntary?",[["An intrinsic principle and knowledge of the end","Correct."],["Feeling and habit","No."],["Freedom from all passion","Passion need not remove voluntariness."]],0)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. The sources of morality: object, end, circumstances",html:`<p>\"A fourfold goodness may be considered in a human action\" (q.18 a.4): as an action (it has being), by its species (from its object), by its circumstances, and by its end.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Object.</strong> \"An action has its species from its object, as movement from its term\" (q.18 a.2). The object is not the bare physical behaviour but what is chosen, as understood by reason: \"taking another's property against his reasonable will\".</li>
<li><strong>End (intention).</strong> The agent's further purpose. A good act done for a bad end becomes bad (giving alms for vainglory); a bad act is not made good by a good end.</li>
<li><strong>Circumstances.</strong> Who, what, where, by what means, why, how, when (q.7 a.3). They can increase or lessen goodness or malice.</li></ol>
<p>The rule: \"evil results from any single defect, but good from the complete cause\" (q.18 a.4 ad 3, quoting Dionysius).</p>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] Intrinsically evil acts</div><p>Some 20th-century theologians (\"proportionalism\") held that no act can be judged wrong by its object alone, apart from consequences. Veritatis Splendor rejects this: \"circumstances or intentions can never transform an act intrinsically evil by virtue of its object into an act 'subjectively' good or defensible as a choice\" (VS 81). The debate continues over how to describe the object (e.g. Rhonheimer's \"perspective of the acting person\" versus Long's \"teleological grammar\").</p></div>`,
  check:Q("A man gives money to the poor in order to be praised. The act is…",[["good, because the object is good","The end corrupts it."],["bad, because a good act done for a bad end becomes bad","Correct: q.18 a.4."],["morally neutral","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Conscience, including erroneous conscience",html:`<p>Conscience is not a feeling but an act: \"an application of knowledge to action\" (q.19 a.5, citing I q.79 a.13). It applies the first principles held by synderesis (P4) to particular cases, through prudence.</p>
<p><strong>Does an erring conscience bind?</strong> Yes: \"every will at variance with reason, whether right or erring, is always evil\" (q.19 a.5). If my reason, however mistaken, tells me something is wrong, I sin by doing it, because I will what I believe evil.</p>
<p><strong>Does it excuse?</strong> Only sometimes (q.19 a.6). If the error is <em>voluntary</em> (I could and should have known), acting on it is still sinful. If the error is <em>involuntary</em> (invincible ignorance, not due to negligence), acting on it is excused. Thomas's example: a man who sleeps with another's wife thinking her his own, through no negligence, is excused.</p>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Two errors</div><p>\"Conscience is always right\" (subjectivism) ignores the duty to form conscience (CCC 1783–1785). \"Conscience must simply obey authority\" ignores that one must never act against a certain conscience, even an erroneous one. The answer is: follow conscience, and form it well.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("If a person acts against his erroneous conscience, according to I-II q.19 a.5, he…",[["does no wrong, because the conscience was wrong","No."],["sins, because he wills what he believes to be evil","Correct."],["is always excused","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Sin: definition and the two rules",html:`<p>Thomas accepts Augustine's definition: sin is \"a word, deed, or desire contrary to the eternal law\" (q.71 a.6). Why the eternal law, and not just reason?</p>
<div class="quote">"Now there are two rules of the human will: one is proximate and homogeneous, viz. the human reason; the other is the first rule, viz. the eternal law, which is God's reason, so to speak."<span class="src">ST I-II q.71 a.6</span></div>
<p>Theologians describe sin as an offence against God; philosophers describe it as contrary to reason. Both are true, because right reason participates in the eternal law (natural law, F7).</p>
<p><strong>Mortal and venial.</strong> Mortal sin destroys charity, the principle of spiritual life; venial sin disorders while charity remains (q.88 a.1; see Questions: \"mortal and venial sin\"). Capital vices (pride as their root; vainglory, envy, anger, sloth, avarice, gluttony, lust) are those from which others spring (q.84 aa.3–4).</p>`,
  check:Q("Why does sin's definition refer to the eternal law and not only reason?",[["Because reason is irrelevant","No."],["Because the eternal law is the first rule, of which reason is the proximate rule","Correct: q.71 a.6."],["Because only Catholics sin","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Grace, justification and merit",html:`<p><strong>Why grace?</strong> Even before the Fall, man needed grace for supernatural good. After the Fall he needs it for two reasons: \"in order to be healed, and furthermore in order to carry out works of supernatural virtue, which are meritorious\" (q.109 a.2). Fallen nature is wounded, not wholly corrupt: it can still do some particular natural goods, \"just as a sick man can of himself make some movements\".</p>
<p><strong>Justification</strong> is the transition from sin to justice by the infusion of grace, with the movement of free will towards God in faith and away from sin, and the remission of sins (q.113 aa.1–8). Trent (Session 6, 1547) defines it as \"not remission of sins merely, but also the sanctification and renewal of the inward man\" (ch. 7).</p>
<p><strong>Merit.</strong> No one can merit the first grace (q.114 a.5). But works done in grace merit eternal life condignly, \"inasmuch as it proceeds from the grace of the Holy Ghost\" (q.114 a.3). Augustine's formula: when God crowns our merits, he crowns his own gifts.</p>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] Pelagianism and its opposite</div><p>Pelagius (c. 400) held that we can be good without inner grace; condemned at Carthage (418) and Orange (529). The Reformers held that justification is God's declaration of righteousness imputed by faith alone. The Catholic position: grace precedes everything, and grace truly transforms us and makes us capable of meritorious acts. The Joint Declaration (1999) shows how much is shared on the priority of grace (see Apologetics).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Can anyone merit the first grace, according to Thomas?",[["Yes, by natural good works","That is Pelagianism."],["No","Correct: q.114 a.5."],["Only the baptised","Baptism is itself the first grace for most."]],1)}
 ],
